VERDICT (AC1 / NEARS-2500 core claim): across 3 login/logout account-switch
cycles (A[remember-ON]->B[remember-OFF, accidental]->B[remember-ON,
screenshot-verified]->A[remember-ON]), the very first cart/list and/or
token-refresh header after every login resolved either the CURRENT
account's own bucket/state or none -- ZERO instances of a previous
account's stale moduleId leaking into a new account's session. This is
the exact exploit path NEARS-2500 fixes (userNumber not yet primed at the
moment saveUserToken's updateHeader resolves the identity-scoped module
cache during the login call itself).

BONUS finding closing prior delta-3 followup: unlike the pre-2500 fix-cycle-3
evidence (ac1-delta3-fix2490-confirmed-no-bleed.log, "OBSERVATION" section),
where A's relogin showed 1-2 calls with moduleId=null/zoneId=<absent>
before settling to her own cached value seconds later, THIS cycle's A
relogin (step 4 above) resolved moduleId=2 immediately on the very FIRST
post-login header (token/refresh at 05:44:59, one second after the 200
login response) -- no null window observed. Consistent with the
userNumber-priming fix also closing that previously-flagged timing gap,
though NOT re-verified with a dedicated multi-repeat test (this was a
single observed instance, not a statistically repeated check) -- noted,
not claimed as a separate formally-verified AC.

Item 2 (non-remember-me interaction check): confirmed via step 2 above
(unplanned but valid: B's remember-me toggle carried over as ON from A's
earlier session and my one tap turned it OFF for B's first login) --
userNumber ended up completely ABSENT from on-device SharedPreferences
after B's remember-me-OFF login, never left holding B's number and never
showing A's stale value either. Matches the fix's documented design: the
priming write is transient (overwritten by the controller's own
clearUserNumberAndPassword() a moment later when remember-me is off).

OBSERVATION (non-blocking, followups[]): AuthController._isActiveRememberMe
is a plain boolean flag toggled only by the checkbox tap (line ~294) --
never reset on logout, never re-initialized per login screen mount. It
persists across account switches on the same device session, so a second
account inherits whatever remember-me state the first account left the
toggle in. This is a UX quirk (checkbox visually reflects it correctly,
screenshots confirm no visual desync), not a security leak and not part
of NEARS-2500's scope -- flagged as a regression-candidate followup, not
a task_bug.

